anymore, because we will work together to analyze charts and learn how

15
00:01:16,740 --> 00:01:19,020
to make a detailed outline.

16
00:01:19,280 --> 00:01:22,780
And we are going to help you with that by looking at

17
00:01:22,780 --> 00:01:28,580
common mistakes that often trap students at band score 5.0, and

18
00:01:28,580 --> 00:01:32,040
what you can do differently to achieve band 6.

19
00:01:32,360 --> 00:01:34,740
Let's look at these four bar charts.

20
00:01:35,180 --> 00:01:38,920
The charts below show the figure for hours per week in the

21
00:01:38,920 --> 00:01:44,160
industrial sector in four European countries in 2002.

22
00:01:44,900 --> 00:01:50,640
Summarize the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and

23
00:01:50,640 --> 00:01:52,860
make comparisons where relevant.

24
00:01:53,200 --> 00:01:56,460
The first mistake is providing no overview.

25
00:01:57,200 --> 00:02:01,180
This is a very important part in your task achievement score.

26
00:02:01,720 --> 00:02:06,680
Many students start describing France's 1-14 hour bar immediately.

27
00:02:07,360 --> 00:02:13,640
To achieve band 6.0, you must provide a summary of the most noticeable features.

28
00:02:14,060 --> 00:02:16,160
So how can we write an overview?

29
00:02:16,840 --> 00:02:18,360
Do you remember the V model?

30
00:02:19,360 --> 00:02:23,720
We learned about it in lesson 1, section 1.

31
00:02:25,020 --> 00:02:26,600
Let me remind you a bit.

32
00:02:27,560 --> 00:02:33,040
It's a two-way thinking method from general to specific and from

33
00:02:33,040 --> 00:02:34,620
specific to general.

34
00:02:36,120 --> 00:02:41,780
Let's use the V model to analyze this question, which involves three steps.

35
00:02:42,600 --> 00:02:43,520
Step 1.

36
00:02:44,510 --> 00:02:48,800
Take an overview of the chart and identify the big three.

37
00:02:50,060 --> 00:02:50,920
Step 2.

38
00:02:51,620 --> 00:02:53,600
Identify the great three.

39
00:02:54,740 --> 00:02:55,600
And step 3.

40
00:02:56,100 --> 00:03:00,040
Go into details and identify the major three.

41
00:03:00,840 --> 00:03:06,320
Alright, according to the V model, in step 1, we need to

42
00:03:06,320 --> 00:03:10,920
take an overview of the chart and identify the big three.

43
00:03:11,830 --> 00:03:14,980
Here, I'm talking about three elements.

44
00:03:15,940 --> 00:03:22,320
The title of the chart, the x-axis or horizontal axis, and

45
00:03:22,320 --> 00:03:25,660
the y-axis or vertical axis.

46
00:03:26,200 --> 00:03:33,260
So we've completed step 1, identify the big three, moving on to step 2.

47
00:03:33,540 --> 00:03:38,060
By looking at the overview of the chart, we will now identify

48
00:03:38,060 --> 00:03:43,920
the great three, corresponding to the three elements, 1.

49
00:03:44,940 --> 00:03:47,740
Subjects of comparison, 2.

50
00:03:48,400 --> 00:03:50,920
Global trends, and 3.

51
00:03:51,600 --> 00:03:52,580
Global features.

52
00:03:53,920 --> 00:03:57,940
For subjects of comparison, we have the following types.

53
00:03:58,580 --> 00:04:01,040
Line graphs include lines.

54
00:04:02,220 --> 00:04:04,480
Bar charts include bars.

55
00:04:05,260 --> 00:04:09,200
These are narrow rectangles and groups of bars.

56
00:04:09,880 --> 00:04:13,420
Tables include rows and columns.

57
00:04:14,580 --> 00:04:18,680
And pie charts, all the sections within the chart.

58
00:04:19,380 --> 00:04:24,840
In these bar charts, the subjects of comparison are bars and groups of bars.

59
00:04:25,050 --> 00:04:29,820
The second element in the great three is global trends.

60
00:04:30,400 --> 00:04:32,260
Trends are the changes over time.

61
00:04:32,900 --> 00:04:37,240
And for charts with trends, this is the most important factor.

62
00:04:37,840 --> 00:04:42,120
So it must be prioritised and clearly defined.

63
00:04:43,420 --> 00:04:47,900
Global trend is the general trend of the graph before we describe

64
00:04:47,900 --> 00:04:49,400
the trend of each line.

65
00:04:50,160 --> 00:04:51,960
I'm sure you still remember.

66
00:04:52,390 --> 00:04:57,580
This is one of the two key components of an overview that

67
00:04:57,580 --> 00:04:59,440
we learned in the previous lesson.

68
00:05:00,380 --> 00:05:06,280
Global trends often include the general trend of all lines, if any.

69
00:05:06,920 --> 00:05:11,840
For example, if all lines in a graph increase, that is also

70
00:05:11,840 --> 00:05:13,880
the global trend of the graph.

71
00:05:14,500 --> 00:05:17,160
And the most significant change.

72
00:05:17,650 --> 00:05:23,500
In this task, your overview should highlight that 36 to 40 hours

73
00:05:23,500 --> 00:05:28,920
was most common for three countries, while France was the exception, with

74
00:05:28,920 --> 00:05:32,500
31 to 35 hours being the peak.

75
00:05:32,920 --> 00:05:37,680
The second common mistake is providing an overview with numbers.

76
00:05:38,380 --> 00:05:43,420
Exact figures like 70% for Denmark belong in the body.

77
00:05:43,580 --> 00:05:46,740
The overview should use general descriptors.

78
00:05:47,260 --> 00:05:53,180
Instead of saying French workers peaked at 40%, say they were most

79
00:05:53,180 --> 00:05:58,320
likely to work between 31 and 35 hours.

80
00:05:58,800 --> 00:06:01,920
Mistake three is describing all data points.

81
00:06:02,500 --> 00:06:05,360
Don't list all eight ranges for France.

82
00:06:05,920 --> 00:06:08,500
Instead, group the low-level data.

83
00:06:09,160 --> 00:06:11,640
In step three, we'll go into details.

84
00:06:12,340 --> 00:06:17,460
To analyse a chart and identify some specific details to include in

85
00:06:17,460 --> 00:06:21,280
this outline, which elements do we need to focus on?

86
00:06:22,040 --> 00:06:24,420
Let's take 30 seconds to think.

87
00:06:58,860 --> 00:07:02,820
Some of you might get lost a bit and don't know where to start.

88
00:07:03,740 --> 00:07:05,580
Here are some suggestions from me.

89
00:07:06,580 --> 00:07:09,180
Please answer the major three questions.

90
00:07:09,820 --> 00:07:10,260
1.

91
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:14,740
How should the information be divided into two body paragraphs?

92
00:07:16,060 --> 00:07:16,940
2.

93
00:07:17,520 --> 00:07:21,360
Which individual trends and features should be selected?

94
00:07:22,380 --> 00:07:25,580
It means the trends and features of each line.

95
00:07:26,720 --> 00:07:27,480
And 3.

96
00:07:28,040 --> 00:07:32,120
How should we organise this information to describe the chart?

97
00:07:32,620 --> 00:07:37,000
Therefore, we should group minor data to avoid listing everything.

98
00:07:37,440 --> 00:07:43,420
In the France chart, instead of describing eight bars, we describe only

99
00:07:43,420 --> 00:07:48,520
the peak, 31 to 35 hours, and then group the rest.

100
00:07:49,180 --> 00:07:54,100
We can state that about 10% of employees worked below 14

101
00:07:54,100 --> 00:07:58,940
hours over 50 hours and 41 to 45 hours.

102
00:07:59,400 --> 00:08:02,700
This keeps your writing concise and analytical.

103
00:08:03,240 --> 00:08:05,900
Mistake four is a lack of data grouping.

104
00:08:05,900 --> 00:08:10,240
Don't write four separate paragraphs for four countries.

105
00:08:10,880 --> 00:08:14,940
Use the horizontal approach to group subjects with similarities.

106
00:08:15,860 --> 00:08:21,120
Body one should compare France and Denmark, and body two should compare

107
00:08:21,120 --> 00:08:22,520
Sweden and the UK.

108
00:08:23,220 --> 00:08:26,480
We have the following outline for these bar charts.

109
00:08:34,520 --> 00:08:37,820
Mistake five is using the incorrect tense.

110
00:08:38,280 --> 00:08:41,240
This mistake will poorly affect your score on grammar.

111
00:08:41,240 --> 00:08:44,680
Look at the x-axis to identify when.

112
00:08:45,620 --> 00:08:47,560
These charts are from 2002.

113
00:08:48,480 --> 00:08:50,680
All verbs must be in the past tense.

114
00:08:51,380 --> 00:08:54,940
I suggest you write a big word past on your paper to

115
00:08:54,940 --> 00:08:59,040
remind you to use worked, accounted for, or was.

116
00:09:02,700 --> 00:09:06,020
Mistake six is subject-verb disagreement.

117
00:09:06,600 --> 00:09:11,060
When your subject is the proportion of workers, the verb must be

118
00:09:11,060 --> 00:09:14,380
singular because proportion is singular.

119
00:09:14,900 --> 00:09:16,660
For example, write...

120
00:09:29,860 --> 00:09:33,280
Mistake seven is using incorrect prepositions.

121
00:09:33,920 --> 00:09:39,340
Using prepositions accurately in your sentences is very important in grammar

122
00:09:39,340 --> 00:09:40,960
and lexical resources.

123
00:09:41,800 --> 00:09:46,580
For example, using accounted at 70% is a common error.

124
00:09:47,360 --> 00:09:51,260
The correct collocation is accounted for 70%.

125
00:09:51,260 --> 00:09:58,500
Similarly, use stood at or working between 15 and 45 hours.

126
00:10:00,160 --> 00:10:06,400
Next, mistake eight is excessive repetition, which affects your score on

127
00:10:06,400 --> 00:10:07,580
lexical resources.

128
00:10:08,400 --> 00:10:12,460
Do not use workers or common in every sentence.

129
00:10:13,060 --> 00:10:19,380
Vary your vocabulary by using employees, working arrangement, most numerous, or

130
00:10:19,380 --> 00:10:20,420
working time.

131
00:10:20,780 --> 00:10:26,520
In order to avoid these mistakes and improve your lexical resources score,

132
00:10:26,520 --> 00:10:31,780
you need to read a lot and enhance your vocabulary by learning

133
00:10:31,780 --> 00:10:36,740
graph-specific phrases and practicing your paraphrasing skills.

134
00:10:37,520 --> 00:10:42,460
For line graphs and bar charts, you should learn comparative languages,

135
00:10:42,940 --> 00:10:46,980
languages for describing trends and changes over time.

136
00:10:49,310 --> 00:10:52,910
Mistake nine is using incorrect trend verbs.

137
00:10:53,350 --> 00:11:00,270
In our sample task, these charts are static, meaning they don't show changes over time.

138
00:11:00,790 --> 00:11:03,170
Avoid increased or dropped.

139
00:11:03,670 --> 00:11:09,790
Instead, use comparative verbs like was three times the next highest figure

140
00:11:09,790 --> 00:11:12,330
or followed closely by.

141
00:11:12,530 --> 00:11:16,930
Finally, mistake 10 is writing one sentence per data point.

142
00:11:17,650 --> 00:11:19,310
This makes the writing choppy.

143
00:11:19,750 --> 00:11:22,770
Use relative clauses to combine information.

144
00:11:23,530 --> 00:11:29,570
Instead of two sentences, write, In France, people who worked 31 to

145
00:11:29,570 --> 00:11:35,910
35 hours were the most numerous at 40%, followed closely by those

146
00:11:35,910 --> 00:11:40,410
working 36 to 40 hours at 35%.

147
00:11:40,410 --> 00:11:43,010
Now let's look at our sample writing.

